+// Backend is chosen by DB_PATH (see main): ":memory:" (the test/dev default,
+// gone on exit) or a file that persists.
 //
-// Concurrency v1: ONE shared connection, the RW_Mutex taken EXCLUSIVELY for every
-// op — reads included. A single connection's prepared statements are shared
-// mutable state (bound params + cursor), so two threads stepping them under a
-// *shared* read lock would corrupt each other. Parallel reads come back when we
-// move to per-thread WAL connections, deferred until the load tests demand it
-// (docs/DATA_IMPL.md §4). The RW_Mutex type is kept for exactly that future.
+// Concurrency v3 (store v3): ONE writer connection, guarded by wlock, and one
+// read-only connection per event-loop thread, opened on that thread's first read
+// (a thread_local, so the server needs no thread hook). A loop runs its handlers
+// one at a time, so a reader's prepared statements are never stepped by two
+// callers at once and need no lock, and WAL lets every reader read while the
+// writer writes. A write reads its row back on the writer, under wlock, so a
+// client sees exactly what it wrote. Every connection turns QPSG on before it
+// prepares anything: a bound LIMIT then never re-prepares a statement.
+//
+// ":memory:" comes in two modes (Mem_Mode, MEMMODE in main). A plain in-RAM
+// database is private to the connection that opened it, and SQLite's ways of
+// sharing one (shared cache, memdb) serialise or starve readers, so:
+// - Ephemeral, the default: a WAL database in a temp file that this process
+//   deletes on close and the next boot sweeps if the process died. It runs
+//   the same code path as a file DB.
+// - Single: one in-RAM connection, and every read takes wlock (the v1 model).
 //
 // Allocator discipline: every string handed back is cloned into
 // context.temp_allocator (clone_col), so nothing returned points into a statement
 // buffer a later step/reset could invalidate.
 
